+# Glass instruments: the Skills controller and the Experience radio
+
+The two playable devices are drawn with the same materials as the About
+section's sampler, cassette and player: a frosted cream shell with a dark rim
+over an opaque cream chassis, near-black glass for the screens, dark pads that
+light orange, brushed metal knobs. They reuse the About kit directly
+(`glassShell`, `inner`, `emissive`, now exported from `glass/glass-devices.ts`),
+so a pad here and a pad on the sampler are the same pad.
+
+## How it works: a surface layer behind the live DOM
+
+The controllers' markup already does everything a playable device must (focus,
+keyboard, `aria-*`, audio, the scroll sequence). Replacing it with a canvas
+would throw that away, so the 3D is a **layer behind it**:
+
+- **Layout from the DOM.** Every control carries `data-i3d="kind:id"`. The model
+  is built at those controls' measured rectangles (orthographic camera, one unit
+  = one CSS px), so reflows, breakpoints and key counts need no changes here.
+- **State from the DOM.** A pad is pressed by `aria-pressed`, flashed by
+  `data-hit`, a knob sits at `aria-valuenow`, a fader at `data-v`. A
+  MutationObserver wakes the renderer. React does not know the canvas exists.
+- **Input stays in the DOM.** Clicks, drags, the A-L keys and 1-6 pad shortcuts
+  all work exactly as before; the canvas is `pointer-events: none`.
+- **The DOM is the fallback.** `data-instrument="on"` is set only after a first
+  frame is drawn; the stylesheet hides DOM *surfaces* only then (controls go to
+  `opacity: 0`, never `display`, so they stay focusable and announced). No
+  WebGL, a phone, a lost context: nothing changes.
+
+## 3D and lightweight
+
+- Desktop width only, and loaded only within 600px of the viewport (three.js is
+  a dynamic import; phones never download it).
+- Render on demand: the loop runs while something moves and sleeps otherwise;
+  off-screen hosts draw nothing even while their DOM changes.
+- One cached geometry per box size, instanced speaker grille, pixel ratio capped
+  at 1.5, one context per instrument (two total, one visible at a time).
+- Pointer parallax (about 2.4 degrees) and a key light that follows the pointer
+  put a glint on the rounded edges.
+
+## What is playable
+
+Controller: pads sink and glow orange (and flash on a hit), knobs turn, fader
+caps slide, the wheels roll, keys dip at their front edge, ARP and PWR light.
+Radio: the TUNE dial (click or wheel) steps stations with a sweep of static and
+a tone, the speaker answers a tap, the LED meter climbs while you press it, the
+needle glides, the dials turn. Sounds are made with Web Audio; no assets.
+
+## Files
+
+`components/instrument/`: `instrument-layout.ts` (pure, tested),
+`instrument-core.ts` (renderer, wiring), `instrument-models.ts` (the two scenes,
+tested headlessly), `use-instrument.ts` (lazy mount). `rack-01/radio-audio.ts`.
+`jest.config.js` now lets ts-jest transform three so scenes can be tested
+without WebGL.
